On Thu, Jul 25, 2013 at 1:03 PM, Bob LeChevalier, President and Founder -
LLG <lojbab@lojban.org> wrote:

> MOI on the other hand is already tied to MEX at the full grammar level.
>  So I am not sure what it means to say that you cannot use MEX with MOI.
>  Almost anything can be used with MOI if you first turn it into a sumti,
> and then apply a ME conversion to that sumti.  You can of course turn a MEX
> into a sumti using LI, so "meli MEX" with appropriate terminators should
> work with MOI.  I just tried it, and you need either a lo'o or a me'u to
> terminate the li/me construct before the moi.

I consider ME LI MEX MOI to be a hack, not a proper solution. (Not that it
was a bad idea when the goal was to get a YACC working, but it's a bad idea
now, when the goal is to get a PEG working.) Simply MEX MOI *should* be
fine.

mi'e la latro'a mu'o

